.TH MINSEC.TOML 5 "2026-08-24" "minsec" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
minsec.toml \- minsec daemon configuration and drop-in format
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/minsec/minsec.toml
.br
.I /etc/minsec/conf.d/*.toml
.SH DESCRIPTION
These TOML files configure the
.BR minsec (1)
daemon and its per-filter policy.
The main file is optional; an absent file produces the built-in defaults with
no filters enabled.
.PP
After reading
.IR minsec.toml ,
minsec reads regular files ending in
.B .toml
from
.I conf.d
in lexical pathname order.
Tables are merged recursively.
A later scalar, array, or other non-table value replaces the earlier value.
This makes a small drop-in sufficient to override one setting.
.PP
Unknown keys at every documented table level are errors.
Run
.B minsec check --all
after editing and restart the daemon to apply changes.
There is no live configuration reload.
.SH DURATION VALUES
A duration may be a TOML string containing bare seconds or concatenated
integer/unit pairs, or a TOML integer number of seconds.
Supported units are
.BR s ,
.BR m ,
.BR h ,
.BR d ,
and
.BR w .
Examples include
.BR "\(dq30\(dq" ,
.BR "\(dq10m\(dq" ,
.BR "\(dq1h30m\(dq" ,
and
.BR "\(dq2d\(dq" .
.SH DEFAULTS TABLE
The
.B [defaults]
table sets global policy.
.TP
.BI bantime " = duration"
Initial automatic and default manual ban lifetime.
Default:
.BR 1h .
.TP
.BI findtime " = duration"
Window in which failures count toward
.BR maxretry .
It must be greater than zero.
Default:
.BR 10m .
.TP
.BI maxretry " = integer"
Failures from the same network in the same filter and
.B findtime
window that trigger a ban.
The value must be from 1 through 32.
Default: 5.
.TP
.BI escalate_enabled " = boolean"
Enable increasing lifetimes for repeat automatic bans.
Default:
.BR true .
.TP
.BI escalate " = { factor = integer, max = duration, memory = duration }"
Repeat-offender policy.
Each previous automatic ban still in history multiplies the base ban lifetime
by
.BR factor ,
capped at
.BR max .
History older than
.B memory
is forgotten; that retention applies even when
.B escalate_enabled
is false.
Members omitted from this inline table receive defaults.
Ban history is reconstructed at startup from the event log, so escalation
survives restarts.
Defaults are factor 2, maximum 1w, and memory 30d.
.TP
.BI allow " = [network, ...]"
IP addresses or CIDR networks that must never be banned.
TOML values are strings, for example
.BR [\(dq203.0.113.0/24\(dq] .
Loopback, unspecified, and addresses currently assigned to the local host are
also protected.
A candidate IPv6 aggregate is rejected if it overlaps an allowed network.
Default: empty.
.TP
.BI backend " = \(dqnft\(dq | \(dqnull\(dq | \(dqexec\(dq"
Firewall backend.
.B nft
maintains the project's nftables table,
.B null
makes no firewall changes, and
.B exec
invokes an operator-provided command.
Default:
.BR nft .
.TP
.BI exec_command " = string"
Trusted shell command used by the
.B exec
backend.
It is required when
.B backend = \(dqexec\(dq
and is invoked through
.B /bin/sh -c
with
.BR setup ,
.BR "ban network ttl-seconds" ,
or
.B unban network
arguments.
The arguments are appended as positional parameters, so the value may be a
program path or a shell fragment reading
.BR $1 ,
.BR $2 ,
and
.BR $3 ;
see
.BR minsec (1).
There is no default.
.TP
.BI ipv6_prefix " = integer"
Prefix length used to aggregate IPv6 addresses for tracking and banning.
It must be from 1 through 128.
For example, the default 64 tracks and bans one /64 rather than each /128.
IPv4 addresses are tracked as /32.
.TP
.BI max_tracked " = integer"
Approximate upper bound for in-memory failure trackers and escalation-history
entries.
Idle or oldest tracking entries are discarded as needed; active firewall bans
are not discarded merely to meet this bound.
Default: 50000.
.TP
.BI journal " = boolean"
Prefer the systemd journal for filters that define journal selectors when
journal support is available.
If selected for a filter, journal input is used instead of that filter's file
list to avoid double counting.
If disabled or unavailable, the file list is used.
Default:
.BR true .
.SH PATHS TABLE
The
.B [paths]
table selects runtime and persistent locations.
.TP
.BI socket " = path"
Unix-domain control socket.
Default:
.IR /run/minsec/minsec.sock .
.TP
.BI state_dir " = path"
Persistent state directory.
The event log is stored as
.I events.jsonl
below it, with one rotated generation
.I events.jsonl.1
created when the current file passes 8 MiB.
Default:
.IR /var/lib/minsec .
.SH FILTER TABLES
Each
.B [filters.name]
table enables a built-in or custom filter and may override its policy or input
definition.
The table name is the filter lookup name.
.TP
.BI enabled " = boolean"
Load the filter into the running engine.
Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.BI bantime " = duration"
Override
.B defaults.bantime
for this filter.
.TP
.BI findtime " = duration"
Override
.B defaults.findtime
for this filter.
.TP
.BI maxretry " = integer"
Override
.B defaults.maxretry
for this filter.
The value must be from 1 through 32.
.TP
.BI files " = [path-pattern, ...]"
Replace the filter definition's complete file list.
Shell-style glob patterns are supported.
.TP
.BI journal " = { units = [...], identifiers = [...], comm = [...] }"
Replace the filter definition's complete journal selector.
All member arrays default to empty; selector entries are alternatives, not a
conjunction.
.TP
.BI ports " = [integer, ...]"
Replace the service-port metadata from the filter definition.
The value appears in effective inspection policy.
The current nft backend bans source networks on all ports; this setting does
not restrict its drop rules.
.SH SOURCE SELECTION
When journal input is enabled, available, and the filter has a nonempty
selector, only journal input is configured for that filter.
Otherwise its files are followed.
Journal
.B units
match
.BR _SYSTEMD_UNIT ;
.B identifiers
match
.BR SYSLOG_IDENTIFIER ;
and
.B comm
matches
.BR _COMM .
Unit matches are accepted directly because journald derives them from cgroup
provenance.
Identifier and command matches are accepted only for records from a system UID
(0 through 999), because those fields can otherwise be forged by local users.
.SH DROP-INS
A typical drop-in enables one filter and changes one policy value:
.PP
.nf
[filters.sshd]
enabled = true
maxretry = 3
.fi
.PP
.B minsec enable sshd
and
.B minsec disable sshd
maintain
.I conf.d/sshd.toml
in this form.
Because drop-ins are lexically ordered, use numeric prefixes when several
administrator-managed files may override the same key.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
[defaults]
bantime = "1h"
findtime = "10m"
maxretry = 5
backend = "nft"
escalate = { factor = 2, max = "1w", memory = "30d" }
allow = ["203.0.113.0/24"]
ipv6_prefix = 64
journal = true

[paths]
socket = "/run/minsec/minsec.sock"
state_dir = "/var/lib/minsec"

[filters.sshd]
enabled = true
maxretry = 3

[filters.wordpress]
enabled = true
files = ["/var/log/virtualmin/*_access_log"]
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/minsec/minsec.toml
Main configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/minsec/conf.d/*.toml
Lexically ordered drop-ins using this same format.
.TP
.I /etc/minsec/filters/*.toml
Custom filter definitions, documented in
.BR minsec-filter.toml (5).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR minsec (1),
.BR minsec-filter.toml (5),
.BR minsec-sync.toml (5),
.BR nft (8)
